-**Edge Cases:**
-- **Precision:** Image normalization to `[0, 1]` requires float32 to avoid clipping [S2, unofficial].
-- **Boundaries:** Ensure `mogrify` command handles file extensions correctly (jpg vs png) [S2, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Input:** If `poses_bounds.npy` is missing or malformed, loading fails [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Precision:** Ensure images are converted to float32 immediately after normalization to avoid overflow during training [S1, unofficial].
+- **Boundaries:** The "every 8th image" split assumes the dataset has at least 8 images [S2].
+- **Minification:** ImageMagick must be installed and available in the system PATH [S3, unofficial].
+- **Alignment:** Pose files and RGB files must be strictly sorted and aligned. Mismatched indices lead to incorrect camera poses [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by I/O and ImageMagick resizing. Linear in number of images $N$ and resolution $H \times W$ [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H' \cdot W')$ for storing minified images [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Reduces training time significantly by lowering resolution (e.g., 1/8th) [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Standardized preprocessing for LLFF; reduces VRAM usage [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on external ImageMagick tool; fixed split ratio (1/8) may not suit all sparse scenarios [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of images $N$ for loading and resizing. Image resizing is the dominant cost if `factor > 1`.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H' \cdot W')$ for storing the image array. For LLFF with $N \approx 100$ and $H' \times W' = 378 \times 504$, this is manageable on modern GPUs.
+- **Strengths:** Standardizes input for LLFF-style NeRF implementations; reduces memory footprint by 64x (area) compared to original images.
+- **Limitations:** Relies on external tool (ImageMagick) for resizing; assumes forward-facing scenes (LLFF style).
+- **When to use:** When training NeRF on LLFF, DTU, or similar forward-facing datasets.
+- **When not to use:** For inward-facing scenes (use Blender/NVSR loaders) or when high-resolution input is strictly required without downsampling.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the NeRF paper and subsequent LLFF-specific implementations [S1][S2, unofficial]. The 1/8th downsampling and every-8th-image test split became community standards for LLFF evaluation [S1].
+The LLFF dataset (Local Light Field Fusion) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. [S2]. The specific data loading protocol, including the 1/8th resolution resize and the 1/8th image test split, became a community standard for evaluating NeRF and related methods on this dataset [S2]. The method described here implements this standard preprocessing step.
 
